     15-Oct-2026
Status:
    succeeds # One of [succeeds|failing since <date>|obsolete| ...]
Recommended resolution:
    200x1x1 (as in the paper; 50 and 100 for convergence)
References:
  Duez, Liu, Shapiro & Stephens (2005), PRD 72, 024028 (arXiv:astro-ph/0503420),
  Sect. IV D 1 "Dynamical background tests: gravitational wave-induced MHD waves",
  Eq. (79), Figs. 13-16; analytic solution: Duez et al. (2005), PRD 72, 024029
  (arXiv:astro-ph/0503421), Table I, case 2.

Comments:
  Full GRMHD test: Einstein (special/z4c) + Valencia MHD (hydro, density,
  magnetic with lgr_metric) with backreaction. A linear standing
  gravitational wave h+,x = h0 sin(kz) cos(kt) (TT gauge, geodesic slicing,
  zero shift) in a homogeneous fluid at rest with a uniform oblique field
  excites Alfven, slow and fast magnetosonic waves plus a particular
  solution at the gravitational-wave frequency.

  Setup of the paper: z in (-1,1), k = 2 pi, 200 points, rho0 = 2.78e-9,
  P0 = 1.29e-9, Gamma = 4/3, B0 = (1.09, 8.26, 14.4)e-5 (Gaussian),
  h+0 = hx0 = 1.18e-4, periodic. Their z axis is x here, (x,y,z)_paper =
  (y,z,x)_code, so B_ext = (B0z, B0x, B0y)/sqrt(4 pi) and the wave is in
  gamma_yy - gamma_zz and gamma_yz (initgr='gw_standing' of
  initial_condition/gr_matter). The linear solution depends on the equation
  of state only through rho0 h = e + p and cs^2 = Gamma P0/(rho0 h), so the
  paper's fluid is p = cs0^2 e with cs0^2 = 0.21662 and e0 = rho0 h/(1+cs0^2).

  check.py evaluates the analytic solution, projects the run on its spatial
  dependence (sin kz, cos kz) and compares at z = 1/8, the point of the
  paper's figures; with --plot it draws delta P, the velocity along the
  Alfven eigenvector and along the slow eigenvector (paper Figs. 13a, 15, 16),
  see gw_mhd_waves.png.

Running
-------
  mkdir data
  pc_setupsrc; pc_build
  pc_start; pc_run            (about 1 min, t = 10 gravitational-wave periods)
  python check.py --plot      (numpy; scipy not needed, matplotlib for --plot)

Expected result (max deviation over 10 GW periods / max |analytic|, z=1/8):

                            dP       dv_A     dv_m2    dB       h+
  N=200 (this setup)        1.2e-4   1.3e-4   1.2e-4   1.2e-4   1.8e-4
  N=100                     1.3e-4   1.5e-4   1.2e-4   1.3e-4   2.0e-4
  h0/3                      4.7e-5   5.1e-5   4.5e-5   4.6e-5   6.4e-5
  h0/10                     3.9e-5   4.2e-5   3.8e-5   3.8e-5   5.1e-5
  h0/10, lgr_backreaction=F 4.4e-6   6.4e-6   5.5e-6   5.1e-6   9.2e-6

  The deviations are not numerical: they hardly change from N=100 to 200
  (the numerical solution itself converges at 3rd order: N=50 and N=100
  differ from N=200 by 3.9e-4 and 4.2e-5 in delta v). They are the two
  effects that the linear solution neglects and that the paper identifies
  as the secular drift of its Eq. (80) and Fig. 14: nonlinear terms of
  relative size ~h0 (the part that scales with h0) and the self-gravity of
  the background fluid, which with K_ij = 0 initial data starts to collapse
  (relative ~E t^2; this is the h0-independent part, which disappears for a
  test fluid, lgr_backreaction=F). The paper shows the agreement only in
  plots; the curves here are indistinguishable from the analytic solution
  as well.

  Special case of the paper, Eq. (82): set hplus0=4.31e-5, hcross0=1.61e-4,
  for which the magnetosonic modes and delta P vanish to linear order
  (up to the rounding of the paper's values: delta P is 1e-3 of the general
  case); the code reproduces this with the same relative deviations.
